  "raw_text": "319\n\nNOUN\n \nCONSTRUCT FORM\nS / FORMAS CONSTRUC\nTIVAS\n \nDE SUSTANTIVOS\n\nFor someone who is new to the Miskito language, the construct forms may take some getting used to. However, it\ndoesn't take long to see that they are a very useful feature of the language.\n\nPara alguien que es nuevo en el lenguaje miskito, las formas\n \nconstructivas\n \npueden tardar un poco en acostumbrarse.\n\nSin embargo, no lleva mucho tiempo ver que son una característica muy útil del lenguaje.\n\nOne purpose\n \nof a construct form is to indicate possession of the noun.\n\nThe “\nki\n”, “\nkam\n”, “\nka\n” endings are the\n \nregular ways to do so.\n\nUn propósito\n \nde una forma constructiva es\n \nindicar la posesión del sustantivo\n.\n\nLas terminaciones \"\nki\n\", \"\nkam\n\", \"\nka\n\" son las\n \nformas habituales de hacerlo.\n\nExamples/Ejemplos:\n\nBaibil\n \n–\n \nBible\n \n/ Biblia\n \nBaibilki\n \n–\n \nmy Bible\n \n/ mi\n\nBiblia\n\nBaibilkam\n \n–\n \nyour Bible\n \n/ tu\n\nBiblia\n\nAi Baibilka\n \n–\n \nhis/her Bible\n \n/\n\nsu Biblia\n\nBas\n \n–\n \nboss, employer\n \n/ jefe,\nempleador\n\nBaski\n \n–\n \nmy\n \nboss, my\n\nemployer\n \n/ mi jefe, mi\n\nempleador\n\nBaskam\n \n–\n \nyour boss, your\n\nemployer\n \n/ tu jefe, tu\n\nempleador\n\nAi baska\n \n–\n \nhis/her boss,\nemployer\n \n/ su jefe, su\nempleador\n\nBrin\n \n–\n \ndesire\n \n/ deseo\n \nBrinki\n \n–\n \nmy desire\n \n/ mi\n\ndeseo\n\nBrinkam\n \n–\n \nyour desire\n \n/\n\ntu deseo\n\nAi brinka\n \n–\n \nhis/her\n \ndesire\n \n/\n\nsu deseo\n\nBuk\n \n–\n \nbook\n \n/ libro\n \nBukki\n \n–\n \nmy\n \nbook\n \n/\n \nmi\n \nlibro\n \nBukkam\n \n–\n \nyour book\n \n/ tu\n\nlibro\n\nAi bukka\n \n–\n \nhis/her\n \nbook\n \n/\n\nsu libro\n\nDawan\n \n–\n \nowner, lord\n \n/\n\ndueño, señor\n\nDawanki\n \n–\n \nmy\n \nowner, my\n\nlord\n \n/ mi dueño, mi señor\n\nDawankam\n \n–\n \nyour owner,\n\nyour lord\n \n/ tu dueño, tu\n\nseñor\n\nAi dawanka\n \n–\n \nhis/her\n\nowner, lord\n \n/ su dueño, su\n\nseñor\n\nGâd\n \n–\n \ngod\n \n/ dios\n \nGâdki\n \n–\n \nmy\n \ngod\n \n/\n \nmi\n \ndios\n \nGâdkam\n \n–\n \nyour god\n \n/ tu\ndios\n\nAi gâdka\n \n–\n \nhis/her\n \ngod\n \n/ su\ndios\n\nKangrigisan\n \n–\n \ncongregation\n\n/ congregación\n\nKangrigisanki\n \n–\n \nmy\n\ncongregation\n \n/\n \nmi\n\ncongregación\n\nKangrigisankam\n \n–\n \nyour\ncongregation\n \n/ tu\ncongregación\n\nAi kangrigisanka\n \n–\n \nhis/her\n\ncongregation\n \n/ su\ncongregación\n\nKing lâka\n \n–\n \nkingdom\n \n/\nreino\n\nKingki lâka\n \n–\n \nmy\n \nkingdom\n \n/\n\nmi reino\n\nKingkam lâka\n \n–\n \nyour\nkingdom\n \n/ tu reino\n\nAi kingka lâka\n \n–\n \nhis/her\n\nkingdom\n \n/ su reino\n\nLilura\n \n–\n \nnecklace\n \n/ collar\n \nLilurki\n \n–\n \nmy\n \nnecklace\n \n/\n \nmi\n\ncollar\n\nLilurkam\n \n–\n \nyour necklace\n \n/\n\ntu collar\n\nAi lilurka\n \n–\n \nhis/her\n\nnecklace\n \n/ su collar\n\nMarit lâka\n \n–\n \nmarriage\n \n/\n\nmatrimonio\n\nMaritki lâka\n \n–\n \nmy\n \nmarriage\n\n/ mi matrimonio\n\nMaritkam lâka\n \n–\n \nyour\n\nmarriage\n \n/ tu matrimonio\n\nAi maritka lâka\n \n–\n \nhis/her\n\nmarriage\n \n/ su matrimonio\n\nPamali\n \n–\n \nfamily\n \n/ familia\n \nPamaliki\n \n–\n \nmy\n \nfamily\n \n/\n \nmi\n\nfamilia\n\nPamalikam\n \n–\n \nyour family\n \n/\n\ntu familia\n\nAi pamalika\n \n–\n \nhis/her\n\nfamily\n \n/ su familia\n\nPlis\n \n–\n \nplace\n \n/ lugar\n \nPliski\n \n–\n \nmy\n \nplace\n \n/\n \nmi\n \nlugar\n \nPliskam\n \n–\n \nyour place\n \n/ tu\n\nlugar\n\nAi pliska\n \n–\n \nhis/her\n \nplace\n \n/\n\nsu lugar",
